data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

**Pattern identified:** Timeout enforcement + ThreadAbortException as control flow + ResetAbort

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a timed-out thread and `Thread.ResetAbort()` to survive aborts. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. The replacement is **cooperative cancellation** with `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter`.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource
            .CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw; // external cancellation — propagate as-is
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### What changed and why

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(work)` | Tasks use the thread pool; no manual thread management |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `Task.WaitAsync(token)` | Cancellable waiting (.NET 6+ API) |
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` (implicit via timeout) | Cooperative cancellation replaces forced abort |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | New exception type for cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely | No equivalent needed — cancellation is cooperative |
| `CancellationTokenSource` not disposed | `using var cts` | Prevents timer/callback leaks |

### ⚠️ Critical Pitfall

**Cancellation is cooperative, not preemptive.** If `work` is a long-running CPU-bound operation that never checks the token, it will **keep running** even after the timeout fires. The caller gets a `TimeoutException`, but the work continues on a thread pool thread.

To make `work` actually stop, it must accept and check a `CancellationToken`:

```csharp
// Before: fire-and-forget lambda — won't stop on timeout
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => HeavyCrunch(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// After: pass the token through so work can cooperate
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                   .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
                   .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Caller checks the token at regular intervals
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in bigList)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

### Other pitfalls to watch for

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Sleep` in `work` won't respond to cancellation | Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` |
| Blocking I/O (e.g. `socket.Receive`) ignores the token | Use async overloads that accept `CancellationToken` |
| Forgetting `using` on `CancellationTokenSource` | Always `using` — it owns timers internally |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish timeout from external cancellation |
| Truly uncooperative code (can't modify the work) | Move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` — that's the only .NET 8 equivalent of a forced kill |
